\citation{Hart1996Sphere}
\newlabel{fig:residual}{{6a}{12}{One pixel, worked. The naive index guess is $\figNaive $; the true nearest ring is $\figTrue $, $\figGap $ rings away and outside any small neighbourhood. The two envelopes of Lemma~\ref {lem:envelope} cross the guard band (pink) at $\figLo $ and $\figHi $, proving that no ring outside those $\figSpan $ indices comes within $g$. The Lipschitz rule then visits $\figEvals $ of them (dots), taking long strides while $|h|$ is large and closing in only near the crossing.\relax }{figure.caption.29}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:residual}{{a}{12}{One pixel, worked. The naive index guess is $\figNaive $; the true nearest ring is $\figTrue $, $\figGap $ rings away and outside any small neighbourhood. The two envelopes of Lemma~\ref {lem:envelope} cross the guard band (pink) at $\figLo $ and $\figHi $, proving that no ring outside those $\figSpan $ indices comes within $g$. The Lipschitz rule then visits $\figEvals $ of them (dots), taking long strides while $|h|$ is large and closing in only near the crossing.\relax }{figure.caption.29}{}}
\newlabel{fig:fan}{{6b}{12}{Why no constant works. Triangles, $\theta =0.02$, $96$ directions per radius; the stray is the worst seen out to that radius. The nearest index leaves any fixed neighbourhood of the naive guess and the gap grows linearly, so a budget is a horizontal line that the requirement crosses --- here at $r\approx 500$, a third of the way across the frame. The proven interval grows too, since the rings near a pixel genuinely become more numerous, but the Lipschitz skip keeps what we evaluate well below it.\relax }{figure.caption.29}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:fan}{{b}{12}{Why no constant works. Triangles, $\theta =0.02$, $96$ directions per radius; the stray is the worst seen out to that radius. The nearest index leaves any fixed neighbourhood of the naive guess and the gap grows linearly, so a budget is a horizontal line that the requirement crosses --- here at $r\approx 500$, a third of the way across the frame. The proven interval grows too, since the rings near a pixel genuinely become more numerous, but the Lipschitz skip keeps what we evaluate well below it.\relax }{figure.caption.29}{}}
