\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces With $\theta =0$ the residual of a translated polygon is convex and piecewise linear in $n$, one segment per facet (hexagon, $s=14$). When the drift is below the spacing (blue) it falls monotonically and crosses zero once, on some facet's own segment, so the answer is a linear solve. At the marginal drift $m=s$ (orange) the leading facet's slope cancels and the residual is \emph  {constant} past the crossover: all large indices are exactly equidistant, so no enumeration of finite length can settle them. One evaluation of the constant settles all of them at once.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:convex}{{7}{13}{With $\theta =0$ the residual of a translated polygon is convex and piecewise linear in $n$, one segment per facet (hexagon, $s=14$). When the drift is below the spacing (blue) it falls monotonically and crosses zero once, on some facet's own segment, so the answer is a linear solve. At the marginal drift $m=s$ (orange) the leading facet's slope cancels and the residual is \emph {constant} past the crossover: all large indices are exactly equidistant, so no enumeration of finite length can settle them. One evaluation of the constant settles all of them at once.\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
