\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {One scene, sixteen-fold of zoom, and the price of the floor.}\  The tool's own opening view --- two walking circle families, the project \textsc  {Moir\'e}{} eases into on first load --- at three times its default scale. \textbf  {(a--c)}\nonbreakingspace zoom $4$, $1$ and $0.25$. Nothing is resampled between them: each is the same pair of scalar fields evaluated on its own grid, so there is no mip level to choose, no filter kernel to widen, and no resolution at which the pattern was ever committed. Stroke thickness is a \emph  {world} quantity and is the same number in all four panels, which is why the strokes get thinner on screen as the zoom falls. \textbf  {(d)}\nonbreakingspace the same frame as (c) with the stroke floor of \textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:stroke}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} removed. All four are rendered at one sample per pixel, exactly as the shader does, so (c) and (d) differ in one constant and nothing else. The floor is what keeps the fringe system in (c); without it the sub-pixel strokes are hit or missed and the pattern goes pale and grainy. It is not free --- see the ink it adds, measured, in \S  \ref  {sec:zoom}. (The table there uses a plainer pair, a spiral over rings, since the third rule under test needs a family with a closed-form phase.)\relax }}{16}{figure.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:zoom}{{8}{16}{\figlead {One scene, sixteen-fold of zoom, and the price of the floor.} The tool's own opening view --- two walking circle families, the project \Moire {} eases into on first load --- at three times its default scale. \textbf {(a--c)}~zoom $4$, $1$ and $0.25$. Nothing is resampled between them: each is the same pair of scalar fields evaluated on its own grid, so there is no mip level to choose, no filter kernel to widen, and no resolution at which the pattern was ever committed. Stroke thickness is a \emph {world} quantity and is the same number in all four panels, which is why the strokes get thinner on screen as the zoom falls. \textbf {(d)}~the same frame as (c) with the stroke floor of \eqref {eq:stroke} removed. All four are rendered at one sample per pixel, exactly as the shader does, so (c) and (d) differ in one constant and nothing else. The floor is what keeps the fringe system in (c); without it the sub-pixel strokes are hit or missed and the pattern goes pale and grainy. It is not free --- see the ink it adds, measured, in \S \ref {sec:zoom}. (The table there uses a plainer pair, a spiral over rings, since the third rule under test needs a family with a closed-form phase.)\relax }{figure.caption.32}{}}
