\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Every preset the tool ships, encoded.}\  \emph  {Top:} the rendered superposition. \emph  {Bottom:} the field each one encodes. Every pattern above is two \textsc  {parallel} layers of pitch $5$\,px differing only in one layer's field expression; the labels are the presets of \S  \ref  {sec:exprs}, whose sources are in Table\nonbreakingspace \ref  {tab:exprs}. The dipole is instructive: towards each pole the equipotentials crowd, and past the limit \textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:contournyquist}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} no carrier can resolve them --- so the render collapses into a dense scribble exactly where the field is steepest, which is an honest report. A contour tracer would have emitted confident polylines there.\relax }}{21}{figure.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:contourfields}{{15}{21}{\figlead {Every preset the tool ships, encoded.} \emph {Top:} the rendered superposition. \emph {Bottom:} the field each one encodes. Every pattern above is two \textsc {parallel} layers of pitch $\contourCarrier $\,px differing only in one layer's field expression; the labels are the presets of \S \ref {sec:exprs}, whose sources are in Table~\ref {tab:exprs}. The dipole is instructive: towards each pole the equipotentials crowd, and past the limit \eqref {eq:contournyquist} no carrier can resolve them --- so the render collapses into a dense scribble exactly where the field is steepest, which is an honest report. A contour tracer would have emitted confident polylines there.\relax }{figure.caption.47}{}}
