\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Two things in one difference.}\  \emph  {Top:} the Studio's \textsc  {swirl} field --- the stream function of four point vortices --- encoded by \textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:encode}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}, so the fringes are the streamlines of the flow. No trajectory was integrated, no seed was placed, and the picture is a pair of \textsc  {parallel} layers. \emph  {Bottom:} \textsc  {bumps} against the same carrier, which is a carrier against a warped copy of itself: the classical shadow-moir\'e arrangement, whose fringes are contours of the displacement along the carrier direction. \emph  {Right column:} the same two states under the envelope view (\S  \ref  {sec:envelope}), which is how an author reads a field off the tool.\relax }}{22}{figure.caption.52}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:instrument}{{16}{22}{\figlead {Two things in one difference.} \emph {Top:} the Studio's \textsc {swirl} field --- the stream function of four point vortices --- encoded by \eqref {eq:encode}, so the fringes are the streamlines of the flow. No trajectory was integrated, no seed was placed, and the picture is a pair of \textsc {parallel} layers. \emph {Bottom:} \textsc {bumps} against the same carrier, which is a carrier against a warped copy of itself: the classical shadow-moir\'e arrangement, whose fringes are contours of the displacement along the carrier direction. \emph {Right column:} the same two states under the envelope view (\S \ref {sec:envelope}), which is how an author reads a field off the tool.\relax }{figure.caption.52}{}}
